<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.14.2: Self Check</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00629</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.14.2: Self Check</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5162ef46-b8bb-452f-8dee-ffab9a9332a8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>A singer sold two types of tickets to a performance, seated tickets and standing-room tickets.</para>
<list>
  <item><span id="docs-internal-guid-a13e090a-7fff-0ac0-255a-60f12ce15359"><span>Seated tickets cost $25.</span></span></item>
  <item><span id="docs-internal-guid-a13e090a-7fff-0ac0-255a-60f12ce15359"><span>Standing-room tickets cost $15.</span></span></item>
  <item><span id="docs-internal-guid-a13e090a-7fff-0ac0-255a-60f12ce15359"><span>More than 60 tickets were sold.</span></span></item>
  <item><span id="docs-internal-guid-a13e090a-7fff-0ac0-255a-60f12ce15359"><span>More than $1,200 was made from selling tickets.</span></span></item>
</list>
<para dir="ltr">Which of the following ticket combinations is possible given the situation described here? </para></section>
</content>
</document>
